Expressions
***********

.[perex]
The `expressions` section decides on operators and expressions: strict comparison with `===`, `!=` instead of `<>`, `&&` instead of `and`, `(int)` instead of `(integer)`, Yoda conditions, parentheses where operator precedence is easy to get wrong, and ternary operators that can be written shorter with `??` or `?:`, or not at all.

Most decisions of this section make the code shorter and keep its meaning. The exception is `comparison.loose: forbidden`: it turns `==` into `===`, and code that relied on the loose comparison then behaves differently, so the fix is a risky fix. With [types from PHPStan |/types], DressCode makes the fix by itself where the code compares values for which loose and strict comparison give the same result; elsewhere it waits for your permission in the [`fixRisky` |/configuration#risky-fixes] key.

```neon
expressions:
	comparison:
		loose: forbidden
		notEquals: '!='
	wordLogicalOperator: forbidden
	assignment:
		targetInValue: forbidden
	coalesce: required
	ternary:
		short: required
		ofTrueFalse: forbidden
	doubleNegation: forbidden
```

```php .[before]
if ($status == 'paid' and $total <> 0) {  // The `==` comparison must be written `===`. // The `and` operator must be written `&&`. // The `<>` comparison must be written `!==`.
	$count = $count + 1;  // The assignment must be written `+=` instead of repeating its target.
	$name = isset($input) ? $input : 'anonymous';  // The ternary testing for null must be written with `??`.
	$title = $this->title ? $this->title : $default;  // The ternary repeating its condition must be written `?:`.
	$valid = $total > 0 ? true : false;  // Useless ternary operator, because the condition itself is the result.
	$flag = !!$value;  // The double negation `!!` must be written as a `(bool)` cast.
}
```

```php .[after]
if ($status === 'paid' && $total !== 0) {
	$count += 1;
	$name = $input ?? 'anonymous';
	$title = $this->title ?: $default;
	$valid = $total > 0;
	$flag = (bool) $value;
}
```

Here the `<>` is changed twice: `notEquals` turns it into `!=`, and `loose` then turns that into `!==`.


expressions.cast
================

The type name of a cast.

- `canonical`: `(int)`, `(bool)`, `(float)`, `(string)` in lowercase, never `(integer)`, `(Boolean)`, `(double)`, `(binary)`
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `canonical`, psr12 `canonical`, nette `canonical`, symfony `canonical`, laravel `canonical` · covers `lowercase_cast`, `short_scalar_cast`, `Generic.PHP.LowerCaseType`, `PSR12.Keywords.ShortFormTypeKeywords`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.PHP.TypeCast`, `Squiz.WhiteSpace.CastSpacing` .[decision-facts]


expressions.assignment.targetInValue
====================================

`$a = $a + $b` is `$a += $b`.

Not about the `$a += 1` it may write, which `expressions.assignment.addingOne` writes as an increment.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `assign_null_coalescing_to_coalesce_equal`, `long_to_shorthand_operator`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.RequireNullCoalesceEqualOperator`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Operators.RequireCombinedAssignmentOperator` .[decision-facts]


expressions.explicitPrecedence
==============================

An operand where logical or bitwise operators are easy to misread is parenthesized.

- `required`: always there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `required`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `Generic.CodeAnalysis.RequireExplicitBooleanOperatorPrecedence` .[decision-facts]


expressions.assignment.increment
================================

An increment or a decrement whose value is not read, a statement or a step of `for`; inside an expression it stays as it is.

- `prefix`: `++$i`, `--$i`
- `postfix`: `$i++`, `$i--`
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `postfix`, symfony `prefix`, laravel `postfix` · covers `increment_style` .[decision-facts]


expressions.assignment.addingOne
================================

`$i += 1`, which is `$i++`, or `++$i` by `expressions.assignment.increment`.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `forbidden`, laravel `keep` · covers `standardize_increment` .[decision-facts]


expressions.wordLogicalOperator
===============================

`and`, `or` bind weaker than `=` and are written `&&`, `||`.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `logical_operators`, `Squiz.Operators.ValidLogicalOperators` .[decision-facts]


expressions.doubleNegation
==========================

`!!$a` is `(bool) $a`.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `forbidden`, laravel `forbidden` · covers `no_short_bool_cast` .[decision-facts]


expressions.comparison.loose
============================

A loose comparison, `==`, `!=` or `<>`, is written `===` or `!==`.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `strict_comparison`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.Operators.DisallowEqualOperators` .[decision-facts]


expressions.comparison.notEquals
================================

The operator of inequality.

Not about whether the comparison is loose, which `expressions.comparison.loose` decides.

- `'!='`: `!=`, never `<>`
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `'!='`, symfony `'!='`, laravel `'!='` · covers `standardize_not_equals` .[decision-facts]


expressions.coalesce
====================

`$a ?? $b` written for `isset($a) ? $a : $b` and `$a !== null ? $a : $b`.

- `required`: always there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `required`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `ternary_to_null_coalescing`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.RequireNullCoalesceOperator` .[decision-facts]


expressions.ternary.short
=========================

`$a ?: $b`, written for `$a ? $a : $b` or the other way round where the condition can be read twice.

- `required`: always there
- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `required`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `ternary_to_elvis_operator`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.DisallowShortTernaryOperator`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.RequireShortTernaryOperator` .[decision-facts]

Shortening changes how many times the condition is evaluated, and that is not always harmless: `array_shift($queue) ? array_shift($queue) : null` removes two items from the queue, while the short form removes only one. So only expressions that can be read twice without a side effect are shortened: variables, array items, properties, constants and scalar values. This is where the syntax tree shows its value: PHP CS Fixer marks its counterpart `ternary_to_elvis_operator` as risky, because in a flat array of tokens it cannot tell a call from a variable; here the tree decides.

```neon
expressions:
	ternary:
		short: required
```

```php .[before]
$name = $input ? $input : 'anonymous';  // The ternary repeating its condition must be written `?:`.
$size = $options['size'] ? $options['size'] : 10;  // The ternary repeating its condition must be written `?:`.
$next = array_shift($queue) ? array_shift($queue) : null;
```

```php .[after]
$name = $input ?: 'anonymous';
$size = $options['size'] ?: 10;
$next = array_shift($queue) ? array_shift($queue) : null;
```


expressions.parenthesesAfterConstruct
=====================================

`return ($x);` is `return $x;`.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `forbidden`, laravel `forbidden` · covers `include`, `no_unneeded_control_parentheses`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.LanguageConstructWithParentheses`, `Squiz.Strings.EchoedStrings` .[decision-facts]


expressions.ternary.ofTrueFalse
===============================

`$a > 1 ? true : false` is the condition itself, where it is a boolean.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` · covers `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.UselessTernaryOperator` .[decision-facts]


expressions.comparison.yoda
===========================

A Yoda comparison, the constant on its left side, a comparison of two variables or calls staying as it is.

- `forbidden`: never there, the variable on the left and the constant on the right, `$a === 1`
- `required`: always there, the constant on the left, `1 === $a`, so that an accidental assignment does not compile
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `forbidden`, symfony `required`, laravel `forbidden` · covers `yoda_style`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.DisallowYodaComparison`, `SlevomatCodingStandard.ControlStructures.RequireYodaComparison` .[decision-facts]
